#!/usr/bin/env rexx
/*
   name:    3-100_compileAndUseNetRexx.rxj

   purpose: Demonstrate how to compile and use a NetRexx program embedded as resource
            in this ooRexx program, cf. http://www.netrexx.org or https://www.rexxla.org;
            the NetRexx program does an abmysal amount of array processing (a pure
            interpreter cannot possibly match performance of a compiled program in this
            case) which is fast if compiled and executed by Java; ooRexx in this case
            hosts the NetRexx program and instrumentates it.
            This demonstrates how easy it is to create external compiled classes/functionality
            from ooRexx and directly use it.

   invoke:  "rexx 3-100_compileAndUseNetRexx.rxj"
            ... Rexx script will demonstrate compiling and using an embedded NetRexx program

            "rexxj2 3-100_compileAndUseNetRexx.rxj"      (Windows)
            "rexxj2.sh 2-110_compileAndUseNetRexx.rxj"   (Unix)
            ... Java will load Rexx and execute the Rexx script that compiles and uses
                embedded NetRexx program

   needs:   NetRexx on class or module path and at least Java 8

   author:  Rony G. Flatscher

   Date:    2022-01-13

   Purpose: compiles a NetRexx program and runs it

   license: AL 2.0, at the end of this program
*/

/* A compiled NetRexx program has a static main method that expects an
   array of type String (representing supplied command line arguments).
*/
d1=.dateTime~new
clzNrx=bsf.compile("squares", .resources~squares.nrx, "NetRexx")
d2=.dateTime~new
clzNrx~main(.nil)          -- run the NetRexx program
d3=.dateTime~new
say "compiling NetRexx program:" d2-d1
say "running NetRexx program  :" d3-d2

::RESOURCE squares.nrx     -- NetRexx source code
    /* NetRexx: squares.nrx  */
    options binary    /* in this case we want speed */
    items = 10000000
    s = long[items]   /* define a Java array of type long       */
    loop i=1 to items
       n=long i       /* for multiplication we want to use long */
       s[i-1] = n * n
    end
    /* use a Java stream (introduced with Java 8) 	*/
    say java.util.Arrays.stream(s).max.getAsLong
::END

::requires BSF.CLS      -- get Java support

